<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tribhuwana Wijayatunggadewi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1328">1328</a>–<a href="/years/1350">1350</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Dyah Gitarja, Gitarja, Tribhuwannottunggadewi Jayawishnuwardhani, Dyah Tya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tribhuwana Wijayatunggadewi, also known as Dyah Gitarja, was a Javanese queen regnant and the third monarch of the Majapahit empire, reigning from 1328 to 1350. A daughter of the empire's founder, Kertarajasa Jayawardhana, and his principal wife Gayatri, she had been confined in the palace by her half-brother King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayanegara">Jayanegara</a>, and came to the throne at her mother's order after he was murdered in 1328. In 1331 she led the army against rebels in Sadeng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keta">Keta</a>, and in 1334 she made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> her prime minister; with him she pursued a great expansion of the empire, which conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in 1343. On her mother's death in 1350 she abdicated in favour of her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tribhuwana-wijayatunggadewi.jpg" alt="Tribhuwana Wijayatunggadewi" width="330" height="605" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parvati_Majapahit_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tribhuwana Wijayatunggadewi was born Dyah Tya, according to the Geneng II inscription, the eldest daughter of Kertarajasa Jayawardhana, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, and his principal wife Gayatri. Her half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayanegara">Jayanegara</a> succeeded their father in 1309, and she and her sister Dyah Wiyat received the title Bhre, she as Bhre Kahuripan. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a>, Jayanegara wanted his half-sisters as consorts, which Javanese tradition abhorred, and the council of royal elders led by Gayatri opposed him.</p><p>He then confined the two princesses in the women's quarters of the palace, denying them contact with the court, until they passed the usual age for marriage. After his death, her husband Cakradhara, later Kertawardhana, won a contest for her hand; their children were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>, born in 1334, and Dyah Nertaja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a>, she came to the throne by her mother's order, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayanegara">Jayanegara</a>, who was murdered in 1328, and took the regnal name Tribhuwannottunggadewi Jayawishnuwardhani. She governed with her husband. In 1331 she led the army herself, with her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a>, to crush rebellion at Sadeng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keta">Keta</a>.</p><p>In 1334 she appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> as mahapatih, or prime minister, and he proclaimed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palapa-oath">Palapa oath</a> to extend the kingdom's influence across the archipelago; when his rival Ra Kembar mocked him, a duel followed, warranted by Tribhuwana, in which Ra Kembar died. With Gajah Mada's help she pursued a great expansion: in 1343 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, and in 1347 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityawarman">Adityawarman</a> was sent to conquer the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and the Melayu kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tribhuwana ruled under the auspices of her mother, and when Gayatri died in 1350 she abdicated in favour of her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>. She stayed active in state affairs, serving again as Bhre Kahuripan and sitting on the council of royal elders, the Bhattara Saptaprabhu. After her death a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cremation ceremony was held, and she was posthumously deified as Parvati in the Rimbi temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. Tradition describes her as a woman of exceptional valour, wisdom and intelligence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tribhuwana Wijayatunggadewi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
